<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-chain-conditions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Chain conditions</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            We introduce in this section important classes of modules, viz., Artinian and Noetherian modules. In this section we assume that <m>R</m> is a commutative ring with unity and <m>A</m> is a right <m>R</m>-algebra. For an <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> we denote by <m>S(M)</m> the collection of all <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <definition xml:id="def-artinian-module">
        <title>Artinian Module</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                An <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is said to be <term>Artinian</term> if there are no infinite, strictly decreasing sequences of submodules of <m>M</m>, i.e., if <me>
                    N_1\supsetneq N_2\supsetneq N_3\supsetneq\cdots
                </me>
                is a decreasing chain of <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m> then, there exists <m>\ell\in\N</m> such that <m>N_i=N_j</m> for all <m>i,j\geq\ell</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <definition xml:id="def-noetherian-module">
        <title>Noetherian Module</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                An <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is said to be <term>Noetherian</term> if there are no infinite, strictly increasing sequences of submodules of <m>M</m>, i.e., if <me>
                    N_1\subsetneq N_2\subsetneq N_3\subsetneq\cdots
                </me>
                is a increasing chain of <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m> then, there exists <m>\ell\in\N</m> such that <m>N_i=N_j</m> for all <m>i,j\geq\ell</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
        We often use the following equivalent conditions for Artinian/Noetherian modules.
    </p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-existence-of-minimal-maximal-element">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Following are equivalent for an <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m>.
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The module <m>M</m> is Noetherian (resp. Artinian);
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Every nonempty subset of <m>S(M)</m> has a maximal (resp., minimal) element.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
                Furthermore, <m>M</m> is Noetherian if and only if every <m>A</m>-submodule of <m>M</m> is finitely generated.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <example xml:id="ex-noetherian-but-not-artinian">
        <title>Noetherian but not Artinian</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If we consider <m>\Z</m> as a <m>\Z</m>-module then, <m>\Z</m> is Noetherian but not Artinian.
            </p>
        </statement>
        
    </example>
    <example xml:id="ex-artinian-but-not-noetherian">
        <title>Artinian but not Noetherian</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If we consider <m>\Z(p^\infty)=\{a/p^n:a\in\Z,n\in\N\}</m> as a <m>\Z</m>-module then, <m>\Z</m> is Artinian but not Noetherian.
            </p>
        </statement>
        
    </example>
    <example xml:id="ex-artinian-noetherian-module">
        <title>Module which is both Artinian and Noetherian</title>
        
       <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>V</m> be a finite-dimensional vector space over a field <m>F</m>. Due to dimension reasons, <m>V</m> is both Artinian and Noetherian <m>F</m>-module.
        </p>
        <p>
            Every finite abelian group as a <m>\Z</m>-module is both Artinian and Noetherian.
        </p>
       </statement> 
    </example>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-technical-condition">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>P,Q,R</m> be <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m> with <m>P\subseteq Q</m>. Then there exists the following exact sequence of <m>A</m>-modules.
                <me>
                    0\to Q\cap R\big/P\cap R\to Q/P\to Q+R\big/P+R\to 0
                </me>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-artinian-noetherian-short-exact-sequence">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>0\to N\to M\to P</m> be a short exact sequence of <m>A</m>-modules. The module <m>M</m> is Noetherian (resp. Artinian) if and only if <m>N</m> and <m>P</m> are Noetherian (resp. Artinian).
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-artinian-noetherian-implies-composition-series">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that an <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is both Artinian and Noetherian and that <m>M\neq 0</m>. Then, there exists a sequence of submodules of <m>A</m>
                <me>
                    0=M_0\subsetneq M_1\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq M_{n-1}\subsetneq M_n=M
                </me>
                such that <m>M_{i+1}/M_{i}</m> are simple <m>A</m>-modules for <m>i&lt;n</m>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>
    <proof>
        <p>
            By the assumption we have <m>0=M_0\subsetneq M</m>. Consider the set <m>S_1</m> of all the nonzero submodules of <m>M</m>. As <m>M\in S_1</m>, the set <m>S_1\neq\emptyset</m>. As <m>M</m> is Artinian, using <xref ref="lem-existence-of-minimal-maximal-element"/>, there exists a minimal element, say <m>M_1\in S_1</m>. Note that by the minimality <m>M_1/M_0\simeq M_1</m> is simple. We continue as before by considering the set of all submodules of <m>M</m> containing <m>M_1</m>, so as to obtain a submodule <m>M_2</m> with <m>M_2/M_1</m> simple. Continuing in this way one obtains a increasing chain of submodules
            <me>
                0=M_0\subsetneq M_1\subsetneq M_2\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq 
            </me>
            such that <m>M_{i+1}/M_i</m> is simple. Now Noetherian hypothesis implies that there exists <m>\ell\in\N</m> such that <m>M_\ell=M_i</m> for <m>i\geq\ell</m>. It follows that <m>M_\ell=M</m>.
            
        </p>
    </proof>
    <definition xml:id="def-composition-series">
        <title>Composition series and composition factors</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                A chain of <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m>, <m>0=M_0\subsetneq M_1\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq M_n=M</m>  is called a <term>composition series of <m>M</m></term> if <m>M_{i+1}/M_i</m> is simple for <m>i&lt;n</m>. The factor modules <m>M_{i+1}/M_i</m> are called <term>composition factors</term> of the series.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-Jordan-Holder-for-modules">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>0=M_0\subsetneq M_1\subsetneq M_2\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq M_n=M</m> and <m>0=M_0\subsetneq M_1\subsetneq M_2\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq M'_k=M</m> are composition series of <m>M</m> then, <m>n=k</m> and there is a permutation <m>\sigma\in S_n</m> such that <m>M_{j+1}/M_j\simeq M_{\sigma(j+1)}/M_{\sigma(j)}</m> for all <m>j&lt;n</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    
</section>